---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

/* Single-sourced so the landing page can quote the entry price without
 * risking drift. See src/data/pricing.mjs. */
import { startupTier, tiers } from '../data/pricing.mjs';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Pricing"
  description={`The complete SDK is free under AGPLv3. Commercial production rights for proprietary products start at ${startupTier.price} ${startupTier.period}.`}
>
  <header class="page-hero">
    <div class="container measure">
      <p class="label">Pricing</p>
      <h1>Free under AGPLv3. Priced when you need it closed.</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        There is no trial, no feature gate, and no usage metering. The complete SDK is available
        under AGPLv3 today. You buy a commercial license only when your product cannot meet the
        AGPLv3 obligations.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="cta-primary" href="https://docs.open-e2ee.dev/start/quickstart">
          <span class="oe-button">Build your first encrypted message</span>
          <span class="cta-sublabel">two clients · no account · no server</span>
        </a>
        <a class="oe-button oe-button-secondary" href="/licensing">Understand AGPLv3 use</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="container">
      <ul class="tiers">
        {tiers.map((tier) => (
          <li class="tier">
            <div class="tier-name">
              <h2>{tier.name}</h2>
              <p class="tier-price">
                {tier.price}
                {tier.period && <span>{tier.period}</span>}
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="tier-body">
              <p>{tier.body}</p>
              <p class="muted">{tier.detail}</p>
              <p class="text-links">
                <a href={tier.cta.href}>{tier.cta.label}</a>
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p class="muted measure">
        Taxes may apply. Prices are the annual list prices for the scope named above. Startup
        renews annually until you cancel before the renewal date. The agreement — the{' '}
        <a href="/legal/terms">commercial terms</a> you accept at Startup checkout, or a signed
        order form for Growth and Enterprise — controls the grant, covered products, support,
        renewal, and post-expiration rights. Not this page.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-surface">
    <div class="container measure prose">
      <h2>What a breach looks like afterwards</h2>
      <p>
        The usual reason teams reach for protocol-level encryption is not a regulation. Almost no
        regulation requires end-to-end encryption; HIPAA, SOC 2, GDPR, and the rest are satisfied by
        transport security and encryption at rest. Anyone telling you otherwise is selling
        something.
      </p>
      <p>
        The real argument is what happens on the day your server is breached. If an attacker reaches
        your database and the message bodies there are ciphertext you cannot decrypt either, the
        conversation that follows is a different conversation. Several breach-notification regimes
        treat encrypted data differently from readable data — California&rsquo;s notification statute
        and Article 34(3)(a) of the GDPR both turn on whether the exposed data was rendered
        unintelligible.
      </p>
      <p>
        We are not your lawyers and this is not legal advice; whether any specific regime applies to
        you depends on your data, your jurisdiction, and your facts. But the engineering position is
        straightforward and worth stating plainly: you cannot disclose what you were never able to
        read.
      </p>
      <p class="honesty">
        This does not extend to metadata. Your relay still holds who talked to whom and when, and
        that is disclosable. <a href="/security">The full list is here.</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="band-head">
        <h2>How buying works</h2>
      </div>
      <dl class="definition-rows">
        <div>
          <dt>Startup</dt>
          <dd>
            Sign in, name the legal entity and the covered product, accept the commercial terms, and
            complete annual checkout. No call required. Checkout records which version of the terms
            you accepted, and that version keeps governing your license through renewals.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Growth and Enterprise</dt>
          <dd>
            Portfolio rights, redistribution, integration support, security review, and service
            levels need scoping first. A short meeting, then a signed order form.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Evaluating</dt>
          <dd>
            Build the integration under AGPLv3 first. Nothing about the code changes when you buy a
            license — only the rights do.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <p class="text-links">
        <a href="/legal/terms">Review Startup terms</a>
        <a href="mailto:licensing@open-e2ee.dev?subject=Commercial%20licensing%20question">
          Ask a licensing question
        </a>
        <a href="/security">Read the security model</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
